  "answer": "I cannot say whether ‘this connotation’ warrants rejection of a particular application; the trade mark, claimed connotation and specification have not been provided.\n\n**Act — statutory test:** Section 43 asks whether use of the trade mark for the particular goods or services would, because of its connotation, be likely to deceive or cause confusion. The question is therefore not merely whether the connotation is ‘strong’, but whether it produces that likelihood in relation to the specified goods or services [TMA1995/s43]. Section 33 requires acceptance unless the Registrar is satisfied that the application does not comply with the Act or that a statutory ground for rejection exists [TMA1995/s33].\n\n**Manual — examination practice, not legislation:** A mere possibility of deception or confusion at an indeterminate time is insufficient; assess the application in the current marketplace [TMM/Part29/3/3/2]. The Manual identifies a connotation directly contradicted by the specification as presenting a high likelihood of deception or confusion. It also permits consideration of whether a clear connotation would be understood as fanciful or unrealistic [TMM/Part29/4#1].\n\nThose are the supplied criteria, but the passages and information supplied do not establish whether the unspecified connotation meets them."
